{"date": "2026-11-06", "post_id": 1508668, "title": "A Farewell to Swift", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-6-a-farewell-to-swift/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> It is time to say goodbye to <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a>'s <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/swift/\">Neil Gehrels Swift Observatory</a>. After nearly 22 years of service, the satellite <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/blogs/swift/2026/11/02/nasas-swift-observatory-to-re-enter-atmosphere/\">re-entered Earth's atmosphere</a> on November 4 at 7:39 EST over the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Sumatra\">North Sumatra</a> region of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indonesia\">Indonesia</a>. The <a href=\"https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/12980\">featured image</a> is a mosaic created in 2018 using the first one million <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2009-september-17-ultraviolet-andromeda/\">images</a> captured by the satellite's <a href=\"https://www.swift.psu.edu/uvot/\">Ultraviolet/Optical Telescope</a>. Swift had a combination of instruments, including an <a href=\"https://www.swift.psu.edu/xrt/\">X-ray telescope</a> and a <a href=\"https://swift.gsfc.nasa.gov/about_swift/bat_desc.html\">gamma-ray telescope</a>, that worked together to enable humanity to study, in unprecedented detail, the fastest and most energetic <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-october-17-x-ray-rings-around-a-gamma-ray-burst/\">explosions</a> in the universe. The most recent <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-february-11-a-year-of-sunspots/\">solar maximum</a> accelerated the decay of the satellite's orbit. An <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/swift/swift-boost-mission/\">innovative mission</a> to <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-july-8-swift-boost-mission/\">rescue Swift</a> by boosting its orbit was <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/blogs/swift/\">not successful</a>. Swift burned on <a href=\"https://orbitalradar.com/swift-tracker\">reentry</a> like a shooting star, but the impressive data acquired by the telescope over its mission will continue to bring light to the mysteries of the cosmos for years to come. May it keep inspiring new missions <a href=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1501532358732-8b50b34df1c4\">to boldly go</a> into space, explore new data and seek out new science. <br> <br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong>\u00a0what's next?", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a>/<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/swift/\">Swift</a> and <a href=\"https://www.andreaplanet.com/andreamosaic/\">AndreaMosaic</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a>/<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/swift/\">Swift</a> and <a href=\"https://www.andreaplanet.com/andreamosaic/\">AndreaMosaic</a>", "alt": "A mosaic image of the Neil Gehrels Swift Observatory, created from one million images of astronomical objects captured by the satellite\u2019s Ultraviolet/Optical Telescope.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-6-a-farewell-to-swift/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Swift_Millionth_Image_Mosaic.jpg?w=5252&h=3744&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 November 6 - A Farewell to Swift</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-6-a-farewell-to-swift/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Cecilia Chirenti\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: It is time to say goodbye to NASA&#039;s Neil Gehrels Swift Observatory. After nearly 22 years of service, the satellite re-entered Earth&#039;s atmosphere on November 4 at 7:39 EST over the North Sumatra region of Indonesia. The featured image is a mosaic created in 2018 using the first one million images captured by the satellite&#039;s Ultraviolet/Optical Telescope. Swift had a combination of instruments, including an X-ray telescope and a gamma-ray telescope, that worked together to enable humanity to study, in unprecedented detail, the fastest and most energetic explosions in the universe. The most recent solar maximum accelerated the decay of the satellite&#039;s orbit. An innovative mission to rescue Swift by boosting its orbit was not successful. Swift burned on reentry like a shooting star, but the impressive data acquired by the telescope over its mission will continue to bring light to the mysteries of the cosmos for years to come. May it keep inspiring new missions to boldly go into space, explore new data and seek out new science.  Tomorrow&#039;s picture:\u00a0what&#039;s next?\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"a farewell to swift\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"A Farewell to Swift\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-6-a-farewell-to-swift/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Swift_Millionth_Image_Mosaic.jpg?w=5252&#038;h=3744&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Swift_Millionth_Image_Mosaic.jpg?w=5252&#038;h=3744&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"A mosaic image of the Neil Gehrels Swift Observatory, created from one million images of astronomical objects captured by the satellite\u2019s Ultraviolet/Optical Telescope.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-11-06T00:05:00-05:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-11-05T18:29:37-05:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"A Farewell to Swift\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Swift_Millionth_Image_Mosaic.jpg?w=5252&#038;h=3744&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 November 6<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Swift_Millionth_Image_Mosaic.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Swift_Millionth_Image_Mosaic.jpg\" alt=\"A mosaic image of the Neil Gehrels Swift Observatory, created from one million images of astronomical objects captured by the satellite\u2019s Ultraviolet/Optical Telescope.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> A Farewell to Swift </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a>/<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/swift/\">Swift</a> and <a href=\"https://www.andreaplanet.com/andreamosaic/\">AndreaMosaic</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> It is time to say goodbye to <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a>'s <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/swift/\">Neil Gehrels Swift Observatory</a>. After nearly 22 years of service, the satellite <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/blogs/swift/2026/11/02/nasas-swift-observatory-to-re-enter-atmosphere/\">re-entered Earth's atmosphere</a> on November 4 at 7:39 EST over the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Sumatra\">North Sumatra</a> region of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indonesia\">Indonesia</a>. The <a href=\"https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/12980\">featured image</a> is a mosaic created in 2018 using the first one million <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2009-september-17-ultraviolet-andromeda/\">images</a> captured by the satellite's <a href=\"https://www.swift.psu.edu/uvot/\">Ultraviolet/Optical Telescope</a>. Swift had a combination of instruments, including an <a href=\"https://www.swift.psu.edu/xrt/\">X-ray telescope</a> and a <a href=\"https://swift.gsfc.nasa.gov/about_swift/bat_desc.html\">gamma-ray telescope</a>, that worked together to enable humanity to study, in unprecedented detail, the fastest and most energetic <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-october-17-x-ray-rings-around-a-gamma-ray-burst/\">explosions</a> in the universe. The most recent <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-february-11-a-year-of-sunspots/\">solar maximum</a> accelerated the decay of the satellite's orbit. An <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/swift/swift-boost-mission/\">innovative mission</a> to <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-july-8-swift-boost-mission/\">rescue Swift</a> by boosting its orbit was <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/blogs/swift/\">not successful</a>. Swift burned on <a href=\"https://orbitalradar.com/swift-tracker\">reentry</a> like a shooting star, but the impressive data acquired by the telescope over its mission will continue to bring light to the mysteries of the cosmos for years to come. May it keep inspiring new missions <a href=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1501532358732-8b50b34df1c4\">to boldly go</a> into space, explore new data and seek out new science. <br> <br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong>\u00a0what's next?\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-5-enceladus-in-silhouette/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nCecilia Chirenti, Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261106/html"}